{"name":"Cadmium Violet","hex":"#7f3e98","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cadmium","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A chemical element (symbol Cd) with an atomic number of 48: a soft, silvery-white metal.","A single atom of this element."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cadmium"},{"term":"violet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant or flower of the genus Viola, especially the fragrant Viola odorata;  similar-looking plants and flowers.","A person thought to resemble V. odorata, especially in its beauty and delicacy."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a violet colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/violet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cadmium","blocks":[{"text":"Cadmium is a chemical element; it has symbol Cd and atomic number 48. This soft, silvery-white metal is chemically similar to the two other stable metals in group 12, zinc and mercury. Like zinc, it demonstrates oxidation state +2 in most of its compounds, and like mercury, it has a lower melting point than the transition metals in groups 3 through 11. Cadmium and its congeners in group 12 are often not considered transition metals, in that they do not have partly filled d or f electron shells in the elemental or common oxidation states. The average concentration of cadmium in Earth's crust is between 0.1 and 0.5 parts per million (ppm). It was discovered in 1817 simultaneously by Stromeyer and Hermann, both in Germany, as an impurity in zinc carbonate."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Cadmium-crystal_bar.jpg/330px-Cadmium-crystal_bar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadmium","wikidata":"Q1091"},{"title":"Violet (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Violet is the color of light at the short wavelength end of the visible spectrum. It is one of the seven colors that Isaac Newton labeled when dividing the spectrum of visible light in 1704. Violet light has a wavelength between approximately 380 and 435 nanometers. The color's name is derived from the Viola genus of flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amatista_Laye_2.jpg/330px-Amatista_Laye_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violet_(color)","wikidata":"Q428124"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Cadmium_cutoff.png/500px-Cadmium_cutoff.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Cadmium_cutoff.png/500px-Cadmium_cutoff.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The cadmium-113 total cross section clearly showing the cadmium cut-off","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACadmium_cutoff.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Friedrich_Stromeyer.jpg/330px-Friedrich_Stromeyer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Friedrich_Stromeyer.jpg/330px-Friedrich_Stromeyer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Friedrich Stromeyer","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFriedrich_Stromeyer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/CadmiumMetalUSGOV.jpg/330px-CadmiumMetalUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/CadmiumMetalUSGOV.jpg/330px-CadmiumMetalUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cadmium metal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACadmiumMetalUSGOV.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/2022cadmium.png/250px-2022cadmium.png?ut